"""Grade 9: faith, revelation, Scripture and Christ, written as a catechist would plan it."""
R = {
'9-1-1': dict(
 idea='Human beings are religious by nature. Our longing for truth, goodness, beauty and love points beyond this world, and reason can reach God as its source. Augustine: “our heart is restless until it rests in you.”',
 hook='Ask students to write privately the thing they most want in life. Then ask: “Once you have it, what do you want next?” Notice the pattern of unending desire.',
 activity='“Wonder journal” (30 min). Students write a one-page reflection on a moment of wonder, longing or gratitude (a sunset, music, friendship). They underline the part that felt bigger than the moment. In small groups they share one sentence and look for common themes. Read Acts 17:22–28.',
 teach=['“The desire for God is written in the human heart” (CCC 27). Humans are “capable of God” and made for communion with him (CCC 27–30). Man is a religious being.',
        'Paul in Athens (Acts 17) begins with the Athenians’ own search: “In him we live and move and have our being.” Wisdom 13:1–9 says that from the beauty of creation we can know its author.',
        'Reason can know God from the world and from the human person: the order of the world, conscience, the desire for happiness (CCC 31–36). It remains limited and clouded by sin (CCC 37–38).',
        'Avoid presenting this as proof that forces belief; it is an invitation to consider.'],
 misc=['“Religion is just a crutch invented by weak people.”', '“If reason can reach God, we don’t need revelation.”'],
 mem='“You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.” (St Augustine, Confessions 1.1)',
 assess='Ask students to explain in a paragraph how human desire points to God.'),
'9-1-2': dict(
 idea='From creation and the human person, reason can know that God exists. Thomas Aquinas’ five ways argue from motion, causation, contingency, degrees of perfection and order. They are arguments of reason, which faith builds on and completes.',
 hook='Show a line of dominoes. “What knocked over the first one? Can the chain go back forever?” Introduce the idea of a first cause.',
 activity='“Test the arguments” (35 min). In groups, read short summaries of two of Aquinas’ ways (motion and contingency). Each group states the argument in plain steps, finds one strength and one objection, and presents. Class discusses what the argument shows (a first cause) and what it doesn’t (the Trinity).',
 teach=['The Church teaches that God can be known with certainty by natural reason (Vatican I; CCC 36). Romans 1:19–20: “his invisible nature… has been clearly perceived in the things that have been made.”',
        'Aquinas’ five ways (Summa I, q. 2, a. 3) are about the existence of God as first mover, first cause, necessary being, source of perfection and ordering intelligence (CCC 34). They don’t describe God’s inner life.',
        'Other paths: the cosmos, the moral law and conscience, beauty (CCC 31–34). These are “proofs” in the sense of converging reasons.',
        'Be honest about limits: arguments do not replace faith and may fail to persuade everyone. Faith knows more than reason can reach (CCC 50).'],
 misc=['“Science has disproved God.”', '“Arguments for God are all just guesses.”'],
 mem='“The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament proclaims his handiwork.” (Psalm 19:1)',
 assess='Ask students to summarize one of the five ways and note one strength and one limit.'),
'9-1-3': dict(
 idea='Faith is a personal adherence to God and assent to all he has revealed. It is a gift of grace, a free act of the human person, and consistent with reason. Doubt is not the opposite of faith but can lead to deeper faith.',
 hook='Ask: “Is trusting a friend reasonable? Is it free?” Both. Faith in God is like trust, but supernaturally given.',
 activity='“Doubt conversation” (35 min). A structured conversation. Read John 20:24–29 and Mark 9:24. Questions: What did Thomas need? What did Jesus give? What is the difference between doubt and unbelief? What helps when I doubt? Students write one honest question for God, and one step to seek an answer.',
 teach=['Faith is “a personal adherence of man to God… and a free assent to the whole truth that God has revealed” (CCC 150). It is a supernatural gift that precedes our decision (CCC 153–155).',
        'Faith is reasonable: there are signs of credibility (miracles, prophecies, the Church’s holiness) (CCC 156). Faith and science do not conflict (CCC 159).',
        'It is also free: no one can be forced to believe (CCC 160, 2106). Doubt can be voluntary (a refusal) or involuntary (a difficulty); the second is not a sin (CCC 2088).',
        'Hebrews 11 lists the faithful. Faith grows through prayer, sacraments and community (CCC 162).'],
 misc=['“Faith means believing without evidence.”', '“Having doubts means I’ve lost my faith.”'],
 mem='“I believe; help my unbelief!” (Mark 9:24)',
 assess='Ask students to define faith and distinguish doubt from unbelief.'),
'9-2-1': dict(
 idea='God reveals himself in history, in deeds and words that explain each other, step by step. He speaks through creation, to Adam and Noah, Abraham, Moses and the prophets, and finally in his Son Jesus Christ, who is the fullness of revelation.',
 hook='Show a photo album arranged chronologically. “What do you learn about a person by seeing how they act over time?” God teaches us himself through history.',
 activity='“Build a timeline” (30 min). Students build a timeline of revelation: creation, Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David, prophets, Jesus, apostles. For each, add the event, God’s self-disclosure (e.g., his name at the burning bush) and the Scripture reference. Read Hebrews 1:1–2.',
 teach=['Revelation is God’s self-communication (CCC 50–53). He does it by deeds and words intimately connected (CCC 53, 2). It unfolds in stages (CCC 54–64).',
        'Christ is the fullness: “No one has ever seen God; the only Son… has made him known” (John 1:18; CCC 65–66). There will be no new public revelation after the apostles (CCC 66).',
        '“Private revelations” (e.g., Fatima, Lourdes) do not add to the deposit of faith; they help us live it (CCC 67).',
        'The response to revelation is the obedience of faith (CCC 142–143).'],
 misc=['“God revealed himself only through the Bible.”', '“Private revelations bind all Catholics.”'],
 mem='“In many and various ways God spoke of old to our fathers by the prophets; but in these last days he has spoken to us by a Son.” (Hebrews 1:1–2)',
 assess='Ask students to place five stages of revelation in order and explain how Christ is the fullness.'),
'9-2-2': dict(
 idea='Sacred Scripture and Sacred Tradition flow from the same divine source and together form one sacred deposit of the Word of God. Tradition is the living transmission of the Gospel in the Church, not a list of old customs.',
 hook='Ask: “How do you know what your great-grandparents were like?” Written letters and stories told within a family. Both are needed.',
 activity='“Two streams” (30 min). Students analyze 2 Thessalonians 2:15, 2 Timothy 2:2 and John 21:25 to find the role of spoken and written teaching. Write a one-page summary: What is Tradition? What is Scripture? How do they relate?',
 teach=['“Sacred Tradition and Sacred Scripture are bound closely together… they flow out of the same divine wellspring” (CCC 80–82; Dei Verbum 9). Together they form the deposit of faith (CCC 84).',
        'The apostles handed on what they received orally and in writing (CCC 75–79). Not everything Jesus said and did was written (John 21:25), and the Church’s living Tradition preserves the whole.',
        'Tradition includes doctrine, liturgy, the Creed, Church Fathers and the Magisterium (CCC 78). It must be distinguished from ecclesiastical traditions, which can change (CCC 83).',
        'Show the difference from sola scriptura: the canon itself was decided by the Church’s Tradition.'],
 misc=['“Tradition is man-made customs that contradict the Bible.”', '“The Church adds new revelation.”'],
 mem='“Stand firm and hold to the traditions that you were taught by us, either by word of mouth or by letter.” (2 Thessalonians 2:15)',
 assess='Ask students to explain how Scripture and Tradition relate and give an example.'),
'9-2-3': dict(
 idea='The Magisterium is the Church’s teaching office, the Pope and the bishops in union with him, who authentically interpret the Word of God. It is a servant of the Word, not above it. Teaching comes with different levels of authority and calls for different levels of assent.',
 hook='Ask: “Who decides what a rule in a game means when players disagree?” A referee. The Church has one for the faith, serving the rule, not making it.',
 activity='“Levels of assent chart” (35 min). Students build a chart: (1) dogmas defined as divinely revealed, requiring the assent of faith; (2) definitive doctrines requiring firm acceptance; (3) authoritative non-definitive teaching requiring religious submission of mind and will; (4) prudential decisions. Give examples at each. Read Matthew 16:18–19 and John 21:15–17.',
 teach=['The Magisterium interprets the Word of God authentically (CCC 85–87). Its authority is exercised by the Pope and bishops (CCC 888–892).',
        'Infallibility: when the Pope speaks ex cathedra, or the bishops in an ecumenical council define a doctrine of faith or morals, they are preserved from error (CCC 891). The Pope is not infallible in everything.',
        'The faithful give “the obedience of faith” (CCC 143). The sense of the faithful also has a role (CCC 91–93).',
        'Dissent and development: doctrine can develop in understanding but not contradict (CCC 94).'],
 misc=['“Everything the Pope says is infallible.”', '“The Magisterium makes up doctrine.”'],
 mem='“He who hears you hears me, and he who rejects you rejects me.” (Luke 10:16)',
 assess='Ask students to describe infallibility and give an example at each level of assent.'),
'9-3-1': dict(
 idea='God is the author of Scripture and the human authors are true authors. Inspiration means the Holy Spirit moved them to write what he wished. The Bible teaches without error the truth God wanted for the sake of our salvation.',
 hook='Present two maps of the same city: a street map and a subway map. “Both are true; they serve different purposes.” Scripture teaches the truth God wanted for salvation.',
 activity='“Two creation accounts” (30 min). Compare Genesis 1:1–2:4 and 2:4–25. Students list differences in order and style and the common teaching (God is Creator; humanity is made in his image; creation is good; marriage). Ask what each form of truth the author intends.',
 teach=['Inspiration: the Holy Spirit is the author of Scripture, with human authors acting as true authors (CCC 105–107). 2 Timothy 3:16: “All Scripture is inspired by God.”',
        'Inerrancy: the books teach “firmly, faithfully and without error that truth which God, for the sake of our salvation, wished to see confided to the Sacred Scriptures” (CCC 107; Dei Verbum 11).',
        'This does not mean the Bible is a science textbook, nor that every statement in any sense is to be taken literally. We must ask what the author intended (CCC 109–110).',
        'The Church also teaches that all of Scripture is a unity because of one divine author (CCC 112).'],
 misc=['“Inspiration means God dictated every word.”', '“Inerrancy means the Bible answers every scientific question.”'],
 mem='“All Scripture is breathed out by God and profitable for teaching, for reproof, for correction, and for training in righteousness.” (2 Timothy 3:16)',
 assess='Ask students to explain inspiration and inerrancy with the creation accounts as example.'),
'9-3-2': dict(
 idea='The Church gives three criteria for interpreting Scripture: attend to the content and unity of the whole Bible, read it within the living Tradition of the Church, and keep in mind the analogy of faith. Alongside the literal sense there are three spiritual senses.',
 hook='Show a passage out of context (a movie quote edited). Ask: “What happens when we remove context?” Scripture needs context too.',
 activity='“Practice on Exodus 14” (35 min). Students apply the criteria to the crossing of the Red Sea: literary form and context (history, liturgical retelling), unity (Isaiah 43, 1 Corinthians 10:1–2), Tradition (Easter Vigil, Baptism), analogy of faith (God saves). Then identify the spiritual senses (allegorical, moral, anagogical).',
 teach=['CCC 112–114 sets three criteria: content and unity, living Tradition, analogy of faith. Complementary: literary genre, the author’s intention, historical context (CCC 109–110).',
        'The literal sense is the foundation (CCC 116). The spiritual senses are: allegorical (how events signify Christ), moral (how to act), anagogical (our heavenly destiny) (CCC 115–117).',
        'A medieval couplet summarizes: “The letter teaches what happened, allegory what you should believe, the moral what you should do, anagogy where you are heading.”',
        'Exegetes are to work “so that, as it were by previous study, the judgment of the Church may mature” (CCC 119).'],
 misc=['“A spiritual reading means ignoring the plain meaning.”', '“The Church forbids reading the Bible critically.”'],
 mem='“Then he opened their minds to understand the Scriptures.” (Luke 24:45)',
 assess='Ask students to apply the three criteria to a short passage.'),
'9-3-3': dict(
 idea='The canon is the list of inspired books. The Church, guided by the Spirit, recognized 46 Old Testament and 27 New Testament books by the late fourth century (Hippo 393, Carthage 397; confirmed by Popes). Protestants removed seven Old Testament books (the deuterocanon).',
 hook='Ask: “Who decided which books belong in the Bible?” Take guesses. “It was not decided in a single stroke or by one person.”',
 activity='“Canon research” (35 min). Students use a timeline of canon formation (Muratorian Fragment c. 170, Athanasius’ Easter letter 367, Hippo 393, Carthage 397, Innocent I 405, Florence 1442, Trent 1546). In pairs, they prepare an answer to a Protestant objection: “The Apocrypha was not in the Hebrew canon.” Use evidence: the Septuagint, New Testament quotations, Christian use.',
 teach=['The Church discerned the canon through her Tradition and the Spirit’s guidance (CCC 120). The 73 books include seven deuterocanonical books (Tobit, Judith, 1–2 Maccabees, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch) and additions to Esther and Daniel.',
        'The earliest Christians used the Greek Septuagint, which included these books. Luther moved them to an appendix in 1534. The Jewish canon was fixed later (c. 90–100 AD), after Christianity separated.',
        'Peter notes Paul’s letters as “Scripture” (2 Peter 3:15–16). The New Testament canon was stable by the fourth century based on apostolic origin, orthodoxy and universal use.',
        'The Church honors Jewish tradition. Discuss respectfully.'],
 misc=['“The Catholic Church added books to the Bible.”', '“The canon was decided at Nicaea.”'],
 mem='“Our beloved brother Paul wrote to you according to the wisdom given him… in all his letters.” (2 Peter 3:15–16)',
 assess='Ask students to give three facts that support the Catholic canon.'),
'9-4-1': dict(
 idea='The two Testaments form one story. The Old prepares for Christ and is fulfilled in him; the New is hidden in the Old. Typology — persons and events that foreshadow Christ — is the key to reading them together.',
 hook='Show the first half of a mystery novel, then the last page. “The ending makes the beginning make sense.” Jesus is the ending that makes sense of the whole story.',
 activity='“Typology table” (30 min). Fill a table: type (Adam, Noah’s ark, Isaac, Passover lamb, manna, bronze serpent, Jonah, Temple), New Testament reference and fulfillment. Add one sentence on what the type teaches about Christ. Read Luke 24:44–47.',
 teach=['“The Old Testament is a preparation for the New” (CCC 128). “The New Testament is hidden in the Old and the Old is unveiled in the New” (St Augustine; CCC 129).',
        'Typology is the Church’s ancient method: Adam is a type of Christ (Romans 5:14), Noah’s flood of Baptism (1 Peter 3:20–21), the manna of the Eucharist (John 6:32).',
        'The Old Testament keeps its own value (CCC 121–123). Marcion’s rejection was condemned. The Church honors the Jewish people’s covenant, which God has not revoked (CCC 839–840; Romans 11:29).',
        'Warn against supersessionism: the Church does not teach that Jews have been replaced.'],
 misc=['“The Old Testament God is angry and the New Testament God is loving.”', '“The Old Testament is obsolete.”'],
 mem='“Everything written about me in the law of Moses and the prophets and the psalms must be fulfilled.” (Luke 24:44)',
 assess='Ask students to give three types and their fulfillments.'),
'9-4-2': dict(
 idea='God forms a people through successive covenants: with Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David and finally the new covenant in Christ. Each covenant expands the family of God, and Jeremiah promised a new one written on hearts.',
 hook='Say: “A covenant is a family bond. Each time God widens it.” Draw circles that grow: family, tribe, nation, world.',
 activity='“Covenant chart” (35 min). Plot the covenants with: partner (family, tribe, nation, kingdom, all), sign (Sabbath, rainbow, circumcision, Law/Sabbath, throne, Eucharist), promise and Scripture. Read Jeremiah 31:31–34 and Luke 22:20 and note the fulfillment.',
 teach=['Covenant with Adam (creation, Hosea 6:7), Noah (Genesis 9), Abraham (Genesis 12; 15; 17), Moses (Exodus 19–24), David (2 Samuel 7), the new covenant (Jeremiah 31; Luke 22:20) (CCC 56–64).',
        'Each covenant widens the circle: a married pair, a household, a tribe, a nation, a kingdom, all peoples (Scott Hahn’s “family” model). Each includes oath, sign and blessings and curses.',
        'The new covenant is “everlasting” (CCC 66). The Eucharist is its sacramental sign (CCC 1365).',
        'Link to the unity of salvation history.'],
 misc=['“Covenants are legal contracts.”', '“God cancelled the first covenants.”'],
 mem='“I will put my law within them, and I will write it on their hearts. And I will be their God, and they shall be my people.” (Jeremiah 31:33)',
 assess='Ask students to chart three covenants with partner, sign and promise.'),
'9-4-3': dict(
 idea='The prophets announced the Messiah: born of a virgin in Bethlehem, a king from David’s line, a suffering servant, entering Jerusalem humbly. Jesus fulfills these promises, and the Gospels show it.',
 hook='Say: “Imagine if someone predicted details of your life 700 years before you were born.” Introduce Micah 5:2.',
 activity='“Study five prophecies” (35 min). Pairs examine Isaiah 7:14 / Matthew 1:22–23, Micah 5:2 / Matthew 2:5–6, Isaiah 53 / 1 Peter 2:24, Psalm 22 / Matthew 27, Zechariah 9:9 / Matthew 21:4–5. For each: context in the original, what the New Testament sees, and a caution (not every connection is literal).',
 teach=['Messianic prophecy is part of the preparation for Christ (CCC 522–524, 702–716). Messiah (Hebrew) and Christ (Greek) mean “anointed one.”',
        'The Gospels use many Old Testament texts to show Jesus’ identity. Some are direct predictions (Micah 5:2), others typological or deeper meanings (Hosea 11:1 / Matthew 2:15).',
        'Isaiah 53 is read by the Church as the Servant who suffers for sins (CCC 601). Psalm 22 begins with the cry Jesus uttered on the Cross.',
        'Be fair to Jewish readings of these passages and to critical questions about translation (e.g., almah in Isaiah 7:14 translated parthenos in the Septuagint).'],
 misc=['“Prophecy is only prediction.”', '“Jesus merely staged the fulfillments.”'],
 mem='“But you, O Bethlehem Ephrathah, who are too little to be among the clans of Judah, from you shall come forth for me one who is to be ruler in Israel.” (Micah 5:2)',
 assess='Ask students to present two prophecies with their fulfillments and one caution.'),
'9-5-1': dict(
 idea='The Gospels are reliable historical witnesses to the Jesus who lived. Beyond the Gospels, ancient writers such as Tacitus, Josephus and Pliny mention him. The evangelists wrote within decades of the events, using eyewitness testimony.',
 hook='Ask: “How do historians know about Julius Caesar?” By documents, manuscripts and other sources. Apply the same tools to Jesus.',
 activity='“External testimony” (30 min). Students read short excerpts from Tacitus (Annals 15.44), Josephus (Antiquities 18.3 and 20.9), Pliny the Younger (Letter 10.96), and compare them to Gospel details. Make a list of what independent writers confirm (crucified under Pilate, followers called Christians, worshiped as God).',
 teach=['The Church holds that the Gospels “faithfully hand on what Jesus … did and taught for the eternal salvation of men” (CCC 126; Dei Verbum 19). They are history shaped by faith.',
        'Manuscript evidence: thousands of early copies, the earliest fragments within decades (P52, c. 125 AD). The Gospels were written within about 30–70 years of the events, while eyewitnesses lived (Luke 1:1–4).',
        'Non-Christian sources: Tacitus, Josephus (parts debated), Pliny, Suetonius. Few scholars today doubt that Jesus existed and was crucified under Pilate.',
        'Faith is not just history; but faith is not against history.'],
 misc=['“There’s no evidence outside the Bible that Jesus existed.”', '“The Gospels were written centuries later.”'],
 mem='“It seemed good to me also, having followed all things closely for some time past, to write an orderly account for you.” (Luke 1:3)',
 assess='Ask students to present three pieces of evidence for the historical Jesus.'),
'9-5-2': dict(
 idea='The Church teaches that Jesus Christ is true God and true man, one divine Person with two natures, human and divine, without confusion or division. Councils from Nicaea to Chalcedon defended this against heresies.',
 hook='Read Philippians 2:5–11. Ask: “What does it mean that he ‘emptied himself’ and still remained God?”',
 activity='“Councils and heresies” (35 min). Match each heresy to the council and the truth defended: Arianism (Nicaea 325), Apollinarianism (Constantinople 381), Nestorianism (Ephesus 431), Monophysitism (Chalcedon 451), Monothelitism (Constantinople III 681). Write a one-sentence summary of Chalcedon’s formula.',
 teach=['Chalcedon (451): “one and the same Christ, Son, Lord, only-begotten, in two natures, without confusion, without change, without division, without separation” (CCC 467).',
        'Jesus has a human body, soul, intellect and will (CCC 470–483). He is not a human person with a divine mask but the divine Person who assumed human nature.',
        'The Philippians hymn (2:5–11) describes the self-emptying of the Son. “Emptying” is not losing divinity but taking the form of a servant.',
        'Take care to connect doctrine to devotion: because God became man, our humanity is redeemed (CCC 460).'],
 misc=['“Jesus had two persons inside him.”', '“Jesus only became God after the resurrection.”'],
 mem='“Though he was in the form of God, [he] did not count equality with God a thing to be grasped, but emptied himself.” (Philippians 2:6–7)',
 assess='Ask students to explain Chalcedon’s formula and one heresy it rejected.'),
'9-5-3': dict(
 idea='Jesus’ words and deeds show who he understood himself to be: he forgave sins, claimed to be Lord of the Sabbath, called God his Father, and said “I AM.” Either he was what he claimed or something else; the Church believes he is the Son of God.',
 hook='Read Mark 2:1–12. Ask: “What is the objection of the scribes? Is it reasonable?” Only God can forgive sins.',
 activity='“I AM sayings” (30 min). Collect Jesus’ seven “I AM” sayings in John (bread, light, door, shepherd, resurrection, way/truth/life, vine) and “Before Abraham was, I am” (8:58). Students note which Old Testament images each echoes and which title (Messiah, Son of God, Son of Man, Lord) fits.',
 teach=['Titles: Christ (Messiah), Son of God, Son of Man, Lord (CCC 436–455). “Son of Man” (Daniel 7:13–14) is Jesus’ own preferred title, expressing both humanity and divine glory.',
        'Jesus claims divine prerogatives: forgiving sins (Mark 2:5–7), authority over the Sabbath (Mark 2:28), “I and the Father are one” (John 10:30), calling God “Abba” (Mark 14:36).',
        'C. S. Lewis’ “trilemma” (liar, lunatic, Lord) is a popular argument but should be used carefully, since it omits “legend.” Consider the evidence on the Gospels’ reliability.',
        'Faith in Christ’s divinity is a gift as well as a conclusion.'],
 misc=['“Jesus never claimed to be God.”', '“Jesus is just a wise teacher.”'],
 mem='“I and the Father are one.” (John 10:30)',
 assess='Ask students to list three ways Jesus claimed divine authority and explain their significance.'),
'9-6-1': dict(
 idea='Jesus began his ministry proclaiming, “The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent and believe in the gospel.” His teaching in parables and the Sermon on the Mount reveals the Kingdom and calls for conversion.',
 hook='Read Mark 1:14–15. Ask: “What would it mean if a king’s rule were arriving in your town?” Prepare your life.',
 activity='“Analyze three parables” (35 min). Groups study the sower (Mark 4), the unforgiving servant (Matthew 18:21–35) and the great banquet (Luke 14:15–24). For each: setting, surprise, what it says about the Kingdom, and a challenge. Create a one-minute dramatization.',
 teach=['The Kingdom of God is the central message (CCC 541–546). It is a gift, grows like a seed, and requires conversion (Mark 1:15). It is already present in Jesus and will come in fullness at the end (CCC 2816).',
        'Parables use everyday images to call hearers to decide (CCC 546). Jesus invites all, “especially the poor and sinners” (CCC 543).',
        'The Sermon on the Mount sets out the Kingdom’s ethic (Matthew 5–7).',
        'The Church is the seed and beginning of the Kingdom on earth (CCC 541, 768).'],
 misc=['“The Kingdom of God is only in heaven.”', '“Repentance is just feeling sorry.”'],
 mem='“The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent and believe in the gospel.” (Mark 1:15)',
 assess='Ask students to retell a parable and say what it teaches about the Kingdom.'),
'9-6-2': dict(
 idea='Jesus’ miracles are signs that the Kingdom has come. They show his power over nature, sin, sickness and death, and invite faith. Skeptics such as Hume argue against miracles; Christians respond that if God exists, miracles are possible and testimony can establish them.',
 hook='Read John 2:11 (“the first of his signs”). Ask: “A sign points to something. What does the wedding wine point to?”',
 activity='“Respond to a skeptic” (35 min). Students respond to Hume’s argument (“a miracle is a violation of laws of nature, and testimony is never enough”). In pairs, they draft a short reply: laws describe regular causes, not what God can do; testimony can be strong; probability must consider the evidence. Debate.',
 teach=['Miracles accompany Jesus’ word and show the Kingdom has begun (CCC 547–550). They are “signs” of his identity, not tricks (CCC 548).',
        'Faith is the response; Jesus often asks for it before a healing (Mark 5:34). Miracles do not force belief (John 12:37).',
        'Philosophically, if God exists he can act in the world (CCC 156). Hume’s argument has been criticized as circular. Lourdes and Church-investigated healings continue (CCC 2003).',
        'The greatest miracle is the Resurrection (CCC 647).'],
 misc=['“Miracles contradict science.”', '“Miracles only happened in biblical times.”'],
 mem='“This, the first of his signs, Jesus did at Cana in Galilee, and manifested his glory. And his disciples believed in him.” (John 2:11)',
 assess='Ask students to explain what a sign is and answer one objection.'),
'9-6-3': dict(
 idea='Why did the Son of God become man? To save us by reconciling us to God, to show us God’s love, to be our model of holiness and to make us “partakers of the divine nature.” The Church Fathers: “God became man so that man might become God.”',
 hook='Read John 3:16. Ask: “What would you do for someone you love? What did God do?”',
 activity='“Four reasons” statement (25 min). Students write a statement answering “Why did Jesus come?” in four parts, with a verse for each (John 3:16; 1 John 4:9–10; John 13:15; 2 Peter 1:4). Share and compare.',
 teach=['CCC 456–460 gives four reasons: to save us by reconciling us with God, to show us God’s love, to be our model of holiness, and to make us “partakers of the divine nature.”',
        'Athanasius: “The Son of God became man so that we might become God” (CCC 460) — meaning sharing in God’s life by grace, not becoming God by nature.',
        'The Incarnation is the center of the whole plan of redemption. The New Testament uses many images: ransom, reconciliation, adoption.',
        'Link to the Christmas season and to Easter: one mystery.'],
 misc=['“We become gods by nature.”', '“Jesus came only to teach a moral code.”'],
 mem='“For God so loved the world that he gave his only Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life.” (John 3:16)',
 assess='Ask students to give the four reasons for the Incarnation with a verse each.'),
'9-7-1': dict(
 idea='Jesus died for our sins, in accordance with the Scriptures, as a sacrifice freely offered out of love. The New Testament and the Church use several images — ransom, sacrifice, satisfaction, expiation — none of which exhausts the mystery.',
 hook='Show an object and ask: “What is the cost of love?” A parent’s sacrifice. Look at Mark 10:45: “to give his life as a ransom for many.”',
 activity='“Compare theories of atonement” (35 min). Students compare: ransom (Origen), satisfaction (Anselm), penal substitution, moral influence (Abelard), Christus Victor. For each: the idea, a verse, a strength and a weakness. The Church has not defined one theory; the Catechism uses several (CCC 615–618).',
 teach=['Christ died “for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures” (1 Corinthians 15:3; CCC 599–605). His death fulfills Isaiah 53 and was freely accepted (CCC 609–614).',
        'The Church does not teach that the Father is a cruel judge who punishes an innocent victim. The Father gives his Son; the Son freely offers himself; it is one act of love of the Trinity (CCC 603–605).',
        'Images: redemption (a price paid), sacrifice, expiation, reconciliation (CCC 615–618). The Cross makes possible our salvation and is renewed in the Mass.',
        'Handle “penal substitution” carefully; Catholic teaching emphasizes love and satisfaction rather than a punishment transferred.'],
 misc=['“God needed blood to be appeased.”', '“Jesus’ death was only a moral example.”'],
 mem='“For the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.” (Mark 10:45)',
 assess='Ask students to describe two models of atonement and avoid a common distortion.'),
'9-7-2': dict(
 idea='Jesus rose bodily on the third day. It is both a historical event and a transcendent mystery. The evidence — the empty tomb, the appearances to many people, and the transformation of the disciples — led to the Church’s faith.',
 hook='Say: “If you wanted to prove a big claim in court, you would want witnesses, physical evidence, and motive.” Apply to the Resurrection.',
 activity='“Weigh the evidence” debate (40 min). Teams argue for and against the proposition “The best explanation of the evidence is that Jesus rose from the dead.” Teams use 1 Corinthians 15:3–8, Luke 24, the empty tomb, the appearances, the disciples’ transformation, and alternatives (hallucination, theft, swoon, myth). Conclude with the Church’s claim.',
 teach=['The Resurrection is a historical event attested by signs and also transcends history (CCC 639–647). Paul lists witnesses, including more than 500 at once, most still alive (1 Corinthians 15:3–8).',
        'Alternatives struggle: hallucinations don’t explain group appearances or the empty tomb; theft doesn’t explain the transformation; swoon theory fails given Roman execution.',
        'The first witnesses were women, an unlikely invention in that culture. The disciples were willing to die for it.',
        'The Resurrection invites faith; it is not coercive proof (CCC 644). It is the foundation of Christian hope (CCC 651–655).'],
 misc=['“The Resurrection was only a spiritual experience.”', '“Easter was a pagan spring festival.”'],
 mem='“If Christ has not been raised, then our preaching is in vain and your faith is in vain.” (1 Corinthians 15:14)',
 assess='Ask students to present evidence for the Resurrection and respond to one alternative.'),
'9-7-3': dict(
 idea='Jesus ascended to the Father and sits at his right hand; he will return in glory at the Parousia to judge the living and the dead. Until then, he is present in his Church and the sacraments, and we live in hopeful expectation.',
 hook='Read Acts 1:9–11. Ask: “Why did the angels ask, ‘Why do you stand looking into heaven?’” We are meant to act while we wait.',
 activity='“Creed commentary” (30 min). Students write a short commentary on the lines “He ascended into heaven and is seated at the right hand of the Father; he will come again in glory to judge the living and the dead.” Define each phrase and say how it affects daily life.',
 teach=['The Ascension is Christ’s enthronement in glory and our hope to follow (CCC 659–667). “The right hand of the Father” means authority and honor.',
        'The Parousia, the second coming, will bring final judgment and the fullness of the Kingdom (CCC 668–679). No one knows the day or hour (Matthew 24:36).',
        'In the meantime, the Church lives between Christ’s first and second comings (CCC 671–672).',
        'Resist speculation about dates, as Jesus warns.'],
 misc=['“We can calculate the date of the Second Coming.”', '“The Ascension means Jesus is gone.”'],
 mem='“This Jesus, who was taken up from you into heaven, will come in the same way as you saw him go into heaven.” (Acts 1:11)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the Ascension and the Parousia and how they shape Christian life.'),
'9-8-1': dict(
 idea='Following Jesus means denying ourselves, taking up the cross daily and following him. Discipleship is a response to a call, a relationship and a way of life. The call stories show how Jesus invites ordinary people.',
 hook='Read Luke 9:23. Ask: “What does ‘take up your cross daily’ look like for a teenager?” Honest answers.',
 activity='“Call story reflection” (30 min). Students read three call stories (Matthew 4:18–22, Luke 5:1–11, John 1:35–51). They note what Jesus does, the response, the cost. Then write about a moment they sensed an invitation from God.',
 teach=['Christian life is following Christ (CCC 520, 1694). “If any man would come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross daily and follow me” (Luke 9:23).',
        'All are called to holiness (CCC 2013–2015). The call comes from God’s initiative and requires our free response (CCC 1812–1829).',
        'The cross is not self-hatred but self-giving love. The Beatitudes show the path (CCC 1716–1724).',
        'Discipleship is lived in community, the Church, with the sacraments and prayer.'],
 misc=['“Discipleship means being perfect.”', '“Taking up the cross means seeking suffering.”'],
 mem='“If anyone would come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross daily and follow me.” (Luke 9:23)',
 assess='Ask students to explain what discipleship asks and give a concrete example.'),
'9-8-2': dict(
 idea='We are justified by grace through faith, not by our own works. Grace is a free gift of God’s life that forgives sin, makes us holy and enables good works. We cooperate with grace, and God rewards what he himself accomplishes in us.',
 hook='Say: “A gift cannot be earned. But you can open it or leave it on the table.” Grace is like that.',
 activity='“Clear up misunderstandings” (35 min). Students sort statements: “Catholics think they earn salvation” (false), “Faith alone with no response” (not Catholic), “Grace first, faith responds, works flow” (Catholic). Read Ephesians 2:8–10 and James 2:24 and harmonize. Write a two-sentence explanation of justification.',
 teach=['Justification is “the most excellent work of God’s love” (CCC 1994). It is granted in Baptism and takes away sin and makes us sharers in divine life (CCC 1987–1995).',
        'Grace is a free, undeserved gift (CCC 1996–2005). We cannot merit the first grace; afterwards we merit rewards by grace (CCC 2006–2011). “It is God who works in you, both to will and to work for his good pleasure” (Philippians 2:13).',
        'Ephesians 2:8–10: “by grace you have been saved through faith… for good works.” James 2:24 stresses that faith is alive through works. The Joint Declaration on Justification (1999) with Lutherans shows common ground.',
        'Emphasize hope and humility: salvation is gift and response.'],
 misc=['“Catholics believe in salvation by works.”', '“Once saved, always saved.”'],
 mem='“For by grace you have been saved through faith. And this is not your own doing; it is the gift of God.” (Ephesians 2:8)',
 assess='Ask students to explain the Catholic view of grace, faith and works.'),
'9-8-3': dict(
 idea='We can pray with the Gospels by entering the scene, meeting Jesus and speaking with him. Lectio divina (read, meditate, pray, contemplate) and Ignatian contemplation (imagining the scene with the senses) are two ancient methods.',
 hook='Read Luke 24:13–35 slowly. Ask: “If you were walking beside Jesus on the road to Emmaus, what would you say?”',
 activity='“Lectio divina” (35 min). Guide the class through lectio on Luke 24:13–35 in four movements: read and notice a word; meditate; pray a response; rest. Then try an Ignatian imagination exercise: place yourself in the scene as a disciple. Journal.',
 teach=['Lectio divina: lectio (read), meditatio (meditate), oratio (pray), contemplatio (rest) (CCC 2705–2708). The practice is rooted in the Fathers and monasteries.',
        'Ignatian contemplation uses imagination: see, hear, touch the scene and speak with Jesus (CCC 2708, 2715). Jesus is the one who “looks at us”.',
        'The Jesus Prayer anchors attention (CCC 2616). Gospel prayer should lead to action.',
        'Suggest students pray with the Sunday Gospel during the week.'],
 misc=['“Imagination in prayer is making things up.”', '“Prayer must always be silent.”'],
 mem='“Did not our hearts burn within us while he talked to us on the road?” (Luke 24:32)',
 assess='Ask students to practice a brief lectio and explain the four movements.'),
}
